{
  "id": "i",
  "candidate": "i",
  "heard": "If perplexity sits at the heart of pre-training, is driving it down enough to capture everything we mean by capability? Imagine for a moment that your model knew the true joint distribution of questions and answers. Conditioning on a question would give you the right probabilities for its answers. With a rule for what counts as a good decision, you could act on those probabilities. There is a big distance between that ideal and the models we can actually build. Perplexity puts the same weight on every target token. A predictable bit of grammar takes up exactly as much room in the average as the answer does. Look at this one arithmetic sequence. One model saves enough loss on the earlier tokens to win on the whole line. But at the answer slot, it gives the wrong choice its highest probability. The other model loses on the average and gets that one decision right.",
